{"ex-E401-1":{"number":"1","title":"Can two objects be resolved?","question":"<p>Sophia is observing, with only one eye, the tips of two purple pens which reflect light at 420 nm. The distance between the two tips is 1 cm and they are 70 m from her eye.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b720a7b13ed0a01d2b626a0a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:409.3px\"/></p><p>The diameter of the pupil of Sophia’s eye is 3 mm.</p><p>(a) What is the angular separation of the two tips from Sophia’s eye?</p><p>(b) Show that the two tips cannot be resolved by Sophia’s eye.</p><p>(c) Describe how Sophia should change each of the following quantities to make the two tips resolvable.</p><p>(i) Distance between the two tips</p><p>(ii) Distance of the tips from Sophia’s eye</p>","answer":"<p>(a) θ ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 1.43 × 10<sup>−4</sup> rad</p><p>The angular separation of the two tips from Sophia’s eye is</p><p>1.43 × 10<sup>−4</sup> rad.</p><p>(b) θ<sub>R</sub> ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 1.71 × 10<sup>−4</sup> rad</p><p>Since θ &lt; θ<sub>R</sub>, the two tips cannot be resolved by Sophia’s eye.</p><p>(c) (i) Sophia should increase the distance between the two tips.</p><p>(ii) Sophia should reduce the distance of the tips from her eye.</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>Will the two tips be resolved more easily if the purple pens are replaced by red pens?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>No. Red light has a longer wavelength than purple light. With the same pupil diameter, the diffraction limit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> increases, while the angular separation of the tips stays the same. The tips are therefore harder to resolve.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E401  |  Source PDF p. 18","source":{"name":"E401 Examples.docx","sha256":"ce80e0defa099c487e5415ca02e0a6f0674e1d9c48b28eaf6a84de12786ab730","block":2},"id":"ex-E401-1","chapter":"E401"},"ex-E401-2":{"number":"2","title":"Power of the refracting parts of an eye","question":"<p>For a normal eye, the range of accommodation is from 25 cm to infinity. The smallest power of the refracting parts of the eye is +40 D.</p><p>(a) What happens to the power of the lens when the eye is looking at an object at its near point and far point respectively?</p><p>(b) Find the distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> between the retina and the optical centre of the refracting parts of the eye.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) When the eye is looking at an object at its near point, the power of the lens is the largest.</p><p>When the eye is looking at an object at its far point, the power of the lens is the smallest.</p><p>(b) Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ∞, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = +40 D,</p><p>40 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>∞</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.025 m (2.5 cm)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E401  |  Source PDF p. 24","source":{"name":"E401 Examples.docx","sha256":"ce80e0defa099c487e5415ca02e0a6f0674e1d9c48b28eaf6a84de12786ab730","block":26},"id":"ex-E401-2","chapter":"E401"},"ex-E401-3":{"number":"3","title":"Correction for short sight","question":"<p>Jason is suffering from short-sightedness and is wearing spectacles with lenses of power −1.25 D to correct the defect.</p><p>(a) Figures a and b show the case without and with the spectacles. Complete the paths of the light rays to show how they reach the retina.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c96b88afba0047627dd8648a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.7px\"/></p><p>(b) Find the distance of his uncorrected far point from his eyes.</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e3af6a1fc306773c43e98275.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.6px\"/></p><p>(b) A distant object (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ∞) forms an image at the uncorrected far point</p><p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ?) by the spectacles (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −1.25 D).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>−1.25 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>∞</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −0.8 m</p><p>His uncorrected far point is 0.8 m from his eye.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E401  |  Source PDF p. 32","source":{"name":"E401 Examples.docx","sha256":"ce80e0defa099c487e5415ca02e0a6f0674e1d9c48b28eaf6a84de12786ab730","block":37},"id":"ex-E401-3","chapter":"E401"},"ex-E401-4":{"number":"4","title":"Correction for long sight","question":"<p>The power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of a long-sighted eye is +50.25 D when it is looking at an object at its near point. The distance between the retina and the optical centre of the refracting parts of the eye is 2 cm.</p><p>(a) After wearing spectacles, the near point is corrected to 25 cm. Find the power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the lenses of the spectacles.</p><p>(b) Find the effective power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the corrected eye when looking at an object at its near point.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) An object at the uncorrected near point (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ?) forms an image on the</p><p>retina (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.02 m) by the uncorrected eye (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = +50.25 D).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>+50.25 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4 m</p><p>The uncorrected near point is 4 m from the eye.</p><p>An object at the corrected near point (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.25 m) forms an image at</p><p>the uncorrected near point (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −4 m) by the spectacles (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = +3.75 D</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>= 50.25 + 3.75</p><p>= +54 D</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>Can you find Pₑff without finding P₁?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Yes. Treat the corrected eye and spectacles as a single optical system. An object at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> must form an image on the retina at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>eff</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E401  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"E401 Examples.docx","sha256":"ce80e0defa099c487e5415ca02e0a6f0674e1d9c48b28eaf6a84de12786ab730","block":52},"id":"ex-E401-4","chapter":"E401"},"ex-E401-5":{"number":"5","title":"Refractive index of cladding in an optical fibre","question":"<p>The figure shows an optical fibre consisting of an inner core with refractive index 1.6 and an outer cladding. The minimum angle of incidence for a light ray not escaping at the interface between the core and the cladding is 55°.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/cc44f2be9f6b456565a288a3.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:193.8px\"/></p><p>Find the refractive index of the cladding.</p>","answer":"<p>By Snell’s law,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>1</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>2</sub></p><p>1.6 sin 55° = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin 90°</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1.31</p><p>The refractive index of the cladding is 1.31.</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>Part of the optical fibre is immersed in oil which has a refractive index of 1.47. Will this affect the total internal reflection of light rays in the optical fibre? What if the optical fibre has no outer cladding?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>With intact cladding, total internal reflection at the core–cladding interface is unaffected: the two refractive indices at that interface remain 1.6 and 1.31, so its critical angle stays 55°.</p><p>Without cladding, the oil contacts the core directly. The core–oil critical angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, compared with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> at a bare core–air interface. Only rays with incidence angles greater than 66.7° still undergo total internal reflection in oil; rays with smaller incidence angles can leak out.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E401  |  Source PDF p. 40","source":{"name":"E401 Examples.docx","sha256":"ce80e0defa099c487e5415ca02e0a6f0674e1d9c48b28eaf6a84de12786ab730","block":76},"id":"ex-E401-5","chapter":"E401"}}